>Should I transfer in binary or ascii mode, assuming I get the modem which
>connects to my IBM clone?

There are two different DOS binaries: one for 386's and better with 
lots of memory (nh313386.exe) and one for 286's and better (nh313pc.exe,
I think).  You don't need both unless you want to play the faster 
version on a 386 and have the other one around for your laptop.  
Each is 700000+ bytes, which is well under a meg.  I believe you can
fit both of them on a 1.44 MB floppy.  They're self-extracting 
archive files, so they'll fill a lot more after you extract them,
of course.

The general safety rule I always hear is to set binary mode before
downloading any binary files via ftp.  I forget about once a week.
Never have I received an error as a result.  Maybe I'm just lucky,
so set binary mode. 

BTW, the 386 version is compiled with DJGPP and thus avoids overlays
(fast, but a memory hog) while the PC version is compiled with 
Microsoft C.

Don't even ask about the size of grabbing all the source.  It's 
a lot, but at least you can get it a piece at a time.  

